    "title": "Why Chris Beard gave Nashville steakhouse credit for Ole Miss win vs Georgia",
    "text": "March 12, 2026, 10:42 p.m. CTOle Miss defeated Georgia 76-72 in the second round of the SEC Tournament.Coach Chris Beard motivated a player before a crucial free throw by mentioning a post-game steak dinner.The Rebels nearly lost a 23-point lead before securing the victory.Ole Miss is the first No. 15 seed to win two games since the SEC tournament expanded.NASHVILLE — Ole Miss basketball coach Chris Beard considered a few ways to spend the timeout with 3.9 seconds left.Guard Kezza Giffa, an 83% free throw shooter, had just missed one that would have put Ole Miss up three in the second round of the SEC Tournament. Georgia called a timeout to iced Giffa ahead of his second attempt.\"From a coaching standpoint, do you talk to the guy? Do you not talk to the guy? Do you call a timeout? Do you tell him to get off the line? To me it's always the game-time decision,\" Beard said.Beard pulled Giffa aside for a quick word. Then Giffa swished the free throw. It helped lift No. 15 seed Ole Miss to 76-72 win over No. 10 Georgia.\"I said, 'Kez, look. Big shot here. I really want that shrimp cocktail here in about 45 minutes,'\" Beard said. \"So shoutout to Halls (Chophouse) for helping us get the victory tonight and shout out to Kez for stepping up and making the biggest free throw of the season.\"Ole Miss (14-19) advanced to face No. 2 Alabama (23-8) in the quarterfinals of the SEC Tournament on March 13 (6 p.m., SEC Network.) The Rebels began their run in the SEC tournament by beating No. 10 Texas on March 11.Beard promised his team before the game that, with a win, they would swap the typical hotel meal for a trip to Halls Chophouse. There's a location in Nashville where the 14 oz. filet mignon is on the menu for $85. His steak order? Medium-rare.Ole Miss is the first No. 15 seed to win two games since the SEC tournament expanded to 16 teams before last season.Beard's strategy of keeping the topic of conversation light with Giffa makes sense when considering how the second half played out. The Rebels roared to a 23-point lead with 12:59 remaining.Georgia started making 3-pointers. In a blink, the Bulldogs cut the deficit to 60-58 with 6:14 left. Georgia's full court press contributed to a high-stress situation that, for a moment, had Ole Miss starting to unravel.What was it like in the Ole Miss huddle after the lead nearly evaporated?Guard Travis Perry said everyone was talking, but it was calm. He scored 16 points, his second most all season, and pulled in six rebounds.\"It didn't really need to be said that much,\" Perry said. \"The huddles were all connected, and everybody knew that if we wanted to keep playing we had to win. That was the mindset.\"Ole Miss entered the SEC Tournament having lost 12 of its past 13 games, but has turned it up a notch in the postseason.\"I feel like we're finally starting to break through in these clutch moments,\" forward Malik Dia said. \"In these huddles, not much was said. It was a team effort. Just stay calm, stay poised and get it done.\"The Rebels need three more wins to win the tournament and get an NCAA Tournament bid. Ole Miss hasn't won the SEC Tournament since 2013.Only two double-digit seeds have made the SEC Tournament semifinals. No. 10 Vanderbilt did in 2013 and No. 13 Auburn made it in 2015.Beard said he is not focused on anything other than winning one game at a time.\"My biggest belief, the thing that I'll always remember about this team, is that these guys have not quit,\" Beard said.Sam Hutchens covers Ole Miss for the Clarion Ledger. Email him at Shutchens@gannett.com or reach him on X at @Sam_Hutchens_",
